156	\paragraph{Training-inference gap.} The GAR-ours metric reveals the scale of the distribution shift. Under teacher-forcing, ThreadGate achieves a training GAR of 0.462, but this drops to 0.022 (GAR-Dongre) at inference, a gap of approximately 22$\times$. This gap is reduced from the 31$\times$ observed on training-overlapping data (Table~\ref{tab:gap_ratio}), suggesting that roughly 29\% of the originally observed gap is attributable to data familiarity rather than the distribution shift per se. The remaining 22$\times$ gap on truly held-out data represents the core distribution shift effect.
