239	\section{Analysis}
240	
241	\subsection{NTP-GAR Gradient Competition}
242	
243	To understand why gates fail to learn useful attention interventions, we analyze the gradient dynamics on gate parameters. At three training checkpoints (end of epochs 0, 1, 2), we compute the cosine similarity and magnitude ratio between NTP and GAR gradients on each layer's gate parameter.
244	
245	\begin{table}[t]
246	\centering
247	\caption{NTP-GAR gradient conflict across training checkpoints on Qwen-2.5-7B. Cosine similarity is negative at epoch 0 (indicating opposing gradient directions) and transitions to near-zero by epoch 2 as GAR gradients vanish. NTP gradient magnitude dominates throughout.}
248	\label{tab:gradient_qwen}
249	\small
250	\begin{tabular}{lccc}
251	\toprule
252	\textbf{Checkpoint} & \textbf{cos\_sim (mean $\pm$ std)} & \textbf{Mag ratio} & \textbf{Layers cos $<$ 0} \\
253	\midrule
254	Epoch 0 & $-$0.072 $\pm$ 0.031 & 7.8$\times$ & 22/28 (79\%) \\
255	Epoch 1 & $-$0.038 $\pm$ 0.025 & 11.2$\times$ & 17/28 (61\%) \\
256	Epoch 2 & $+$0.011 $\pm$ 0.019 & 14.1$\times$ & 8/28 (29\%) \\
257	\bottomrule
258	\end{tabular}
259	\end{table}
260	
261	Table~\ref{tab:gradient_qwen} reveals two key patterns on Qwen-2.5-7B. First, NTP and GAR gradients are in opposing directions during early training: at epoch 0, 22 out of 28 layers show negative cosine similarity (mean $-$0.072). NTP pushes gates toward 1.0 (preserving the residual stream for language modeling), while GAR pushes gates lower (attenuating the residual to reshape attention). This conflict is a \emph{predominant} early-training pattern rather than a universal one, as 6 layers show positive cosine similarity even at epoch 0.
262	
263	Second, NTP gradients dominate in magnitude throughout training, with the ratio increasing from 7.8$\times$ at epoch 0 to 14.1$\times$ at epoch 2. This occurs because GAR loss converges quickly (the GAR objective is essentially solved by epoch 0), causing GAR gradient magnitudes to drop precipitously (from $4.4 \times 10^{-4}$ at epoch 0 to $2.4 \times 10^{-5}$ at epoch 2), while NTP gradient norms remain stable at $\sim$3.4$\times 10^{-3}$. The net effect is that NTP completely controls gate parameter updates after early training, pushing all gates toward identity.
264	
265	\paragraph{Per-checkpoint variability.} The gradient conflict signal, while directionally consistent, exhibits substantial per-checkpoint variability. The standard deviation of cosine similarity across layers is 0.031 at epoch 0, meaning individual layers range from strongly conflicting to mildly aligned. By epoch 2, the mean cosine similarity turns slightly positive ($+$0.011) with only 8/28 layers retaining negative values. This temporal pattern indicates that gradient conflict is a transient early-training phenomenon that establishes an adverse initialization bias, after which NTP dominance locks in the identity-like gate values.
266	
267	\paragraph{Llama gradient comparison.} Llama-3.1-8B shows directionally consistent but noisier gradient conflict (Table~\ref{tab:gradient_cross}). Epoch 0 cosine similarity is $-$0.041 $\pm$ 0.048, where the confidence interval includes zero, preventing a statistically robust ``significant conflict'' claim from Llama data alone. The higher noise (std 0.048 vs.\ Qwen's 0.031) is consistent with GQA's shared KV projection averaging out per-head gradient directions. Per-layer correlation between the two models is moderate ($r = 0.43$), indicating that the exact layer-level conflict pattern is architecture-dependent, though the direction of the effect is consistent.
