287	\subsection{Training-Inference Distribution Shift}
288	
289	The order-of-magnitude gap between training and inference GAR is the central empirical finding of this work. We argue that this gap reflects a distribution shift inherent to teacher-forcing: during training, the model optimizes attention patterns conditioned on gold (human-written) conversational context, but at inference, it conditions on its own generated outputs.
290	
291	Attention heatmap visualization provides the most direct evidence. Under teacher-forcing, ThreadGate's goal-token attention at turn 10 averages 0.145 (substantially elevated relative to the base model's 0.038 at turn 1). Under autoregressive generation, this drops to 0.013, which is indistinguishable from the base model (0.011), Uniform Gate (0.012), and LoRA-only (0.014). The four models' inference-time attention patterns are visually identical across all 28 layers, with only layers 0--5 retaining faint goal attention (consistent with shallow layers processing local and positional context that is less affected by distribution shift).
292	
293	The gap ratio analysis (Table~\ref{tab:gap_ratio}) further supports this interpretation. On training-overlapping evaluation data, the gap is 30.6$\times$; on held-out data, it narrows to 21.8$\times$. The 29\% reduction indicates that data familiarity (the model generating outputs closer to gold context on ``memorized'' dialogues) accounts for a portion of the gap, but the remaining 22$\times$ represents the irreducible distribution shift effect.
294	
295	\subsection{Downstream Task Validation}
296	
297	To ground the GAR metric in task-level performance, we evaluate system prompt instruction following accuracy across 20 conversations with 5 tracked instructions per conversation over 10 turns.
298	
299	\begin{table}[t]
300	\centering
301	\caption{Instruction following accuracy across turns (Qwen-2.5-7B). All models show similar degradation rates ($\sim$25\%), and ThreadGate provides no significant improvement over the base model ($p = 0.24$).}
