313	\end{table}
314	
315	Table~\ref{tab:downstream} shows that all models exhibit similar degradation in instruction following accuracy ($\sim$25\% drop from turn 1 to turn 10), and ThreadGate provides no significant improvement over the base model ($p = 0.24$). GAR degradation correlates moderately with accuracy decline ($r = 0.52$, $p = 0.003$, $N = 60$ turn-model pairs), confirming that GAR captures a meaningful aspect of multi-turn degradation. The moderate (rather than strong) correlation suggests that additional factors beyond goal attention, such as in-context learning from intermediate turns and topic drift, also contribute to instruction retention.
316	
317	\section{Discussion}
318	
319	\paragraph{Why residual gating is insufficient.}
320	Our results identify a causal chain explaining gating failure: (1) NTP gradient dominance prevents gates from learning turn-selective interventions; (2) the teacher-forcing distribution shift means that any attention pattern learned during training does not transfer to autoregressive generation; (3) at inference, gates converge to near-identity (2--7\% attenuation), producing transformations too weak to shift attention. These three factors are not specific to our gating design but reflect structural properties of jointly training auxiliary attention losses with NTP under teacher-forcing.
321	
322	\paragraph{Relation to exposure bias.}
323	The training-inference distribution shift we document is related to the broader exposure bias phenomenon \cite{ranzato2016sequence,bengio2015scheduled}, where teacher-forcing creates a mismatch between training and generation distributions. However, existing exposure bias literature focuses on generation quality degradation (e.g., repetition, degeneration). Our contribution is showing that this mismatch specifically and severely undermines auxiliary attention losses: the $\sim$22$\times$ GAR gap demonstrates that attention patterns are far more sensitive to the distributional shift than token-level predictions.
324	
325	\paragraph{Architectural effects: MHA vs.\ GQA.}
326	The comparison between Qwen (MHA, 28 heads) and Llama (GQA, 8 KV heads) reveals that GQA's shared KV projection limits the effective intervention space for residual gating. Training GAR peaks at 0.26 on Llama versus 0.48 on Qwen, and gate reversal is less prevalent (59\% vs.\ 96\% of layers). The higher gradient noise on Llama (cosine similarity std 0.048 vs.\ 0.031) is consistent with shared KV heads averaging out per-head gradient directions. These differences do not undermine the core finding (gating is ineffective on both architectures) but suggest that attention head architecture modulates the severity of the training-inference gap.
327	
328	\paragraph{Constructive directions.}
329	Our findings suggest several directions for addressing multi-turn attention degradation that avoid the teacher-forcing distribution shift:
330	(1) \emph{Inference-time attention intervention}: directly modifying attention weights during generation bypasses the training-generalization barrier entirely;
331	(2) \emph{Decoupled goal-processing pathways}: architectures that maintain a separate channel for goal information, not mediated through the standard attention mechanism;
332	(3) \emph{GAR-aware decoding}: modifying the decoding process to account for goal attention rather than training the model to internalize it;
333	(4) \emph{Exposure bias mitigation}: techniques such as scheduled sampling \cite{bengio2015scheduled} or sequence-level training \cite{ranzato2016sequence} applied specifically to auxiliary attention losses.
334	
335	\section{Conclusion}
336	
337	We investigated whether learnable residual gating can address the progressive decline of goal attention in multi-turn LLM dialogue. Through ThreadGate, a per-layer gating module trained with joint NTP and GAR objectives, we showed that while training-time GAR optimization succeeds (reaching 0.48), inference-time GAR remains at 0.022, an order-of-magnitude gap that persists on held-out data. Four-way ablation demonstrates that gating provides no significant incremental benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning, and partial cross-model replication on Llama-3.1-8B confirms the pattern.
338	
339	The core lesson is that teacher-forcing training creates a distribution shift that fundamentally undermines auxiliary attention losses: attention patterns learned under gold contexts do not survive the transition to autoregressive generation. This finding extends beyond residual gating to any approach that optimizes attention steering under teacher-forcing. Combined with the independent failure of goal re-injection \cite{dongre2026}, our results suggest that addressing multi-turn attention degradation will require inference-time interventions or training paradigms that explicitly account for the generation distribution.
340	
341	\section*{Limitations}
342	
343	Our experiments are conducted on 7--8B parameter models; the failure mode may manifest differently at larger scales where models have greater capacity to internalize auxiliary objectives. The evaluation uses UltraChat dialogues with a maximum of 10 turns; longer conversations or different dialogue domains may show different degradation dynamics. Our downstream validation (instruction following) covers one task type; other manifestations of attention degradation (e.g., factual consistency, persona maintenance) are not evaluated. The Llama replication constitutes partial rather than full cross-model validation, as we train only ThreadGate and LoRA-only (omitting Uniform Gate) due to computational constraints.
