26	\section{Introduction}
27	
28	Large language models exhibit systematic performance degradation in multi-turn dialogue as conversation length increases \citep{dongre2026gar}. This degradation manifests across multiple dimensions: instruction following accuracy drops, goal-relevant information receives progressively less attention, and response quality declines. Dongre et al.\ quantify this through the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR), documenting 27--48\% drops over extended conversations across twelve models, while their residual stream probes reveal that goal information persists (AUC up to 0.98) even as the attention channel closes.
29	
30	This creates a natural hypothesis: if goal information remains in the residual stream but attention fails to access it, a learnable gate on the residual connection should recover it. We test this hypothesis through ThreadGate, a per-layer gating module that modulates residual stream updates based on learned attention steering signals, trained jointly with NTP and GAR objectives.
31	
32	Our investigation yields a detailed taxonomy of failure modes rather than a clean negative result. On Qwen-2.5-7B, ThreadGate successfully optimizes GAR during training (0.02$\to$0.48), but on held-out conversations the training-inference gap is 15$\times$, with inference GAR reaching only 0.032. Four-way ablation (ThreadGate vs.\ Uniform Gate vs.\ LoRA-only vs.\ Base) reveals that gating provides marginal, statistically non-significant improvement over LoRA adaptation alone. We identify three coupled failure mechanisms: (1) \textbf{reverse turn conditioning}---NTP gradients push gates toward identity, causing 96\% of layers to suppress intervention where it is most needed; (2) \textbf{training-inference distribution shift}---attention patterns learned under teacher forcing do not transfer to autoregressive generation; (3) \textbf{insufficient intervention magnitude}---gates attenuate residual updates by only 2--7\% at inference.
33	
34	Cross-architecture analysis on Llama-3.1-8B reveals that both the severity of GAR degradation and the effectiveness of gating are architecture-dependent. Llama-3 exhibits milder GAR decay (48\% vs.\ 71\%), a much smaller training-inference gap (2.4$\times$), and gate reversal in only 34\% of layers. These differences challenge any universality claim but reinforce the value of architecture-specific mechanistic analysis.
35	
36	Our contributions are: (1) a detailed failure mode taxonomy of residual gating under NTP dominance, grounded in a systematic design space exploration (v1--v7) and four-way ablation; (2) quantitative evidence that teacher-forcing distribution shift degrades auxiliary attention loss generalization, with gap magnitudes of 2.4--15$\times$ depending on architecture; (3) cross-architecture comparison revealing architecture-dependent susceptibility to multi-turn attention degradation; (4) a downstream sanity check establishing the partial but imperfect correspondence between GAR and instruction retention.
