39	\section{Related Work}
40	
41	\paragraph{Multi-turn dialogue degradation.}
42	Performance degradation in extended conversations has been documented across model families \citep{dongre2026gar}. Attention dilution, where goal-relevant tokens receive progressively less attention as context grows, is one proposed mechanism. Dongre et al.\ introduce GAR as a quantitative measure and show that goal information persists in residual streams despite attention channel closure.
43	
44	\paragraph{Residual gating and adaptive computation.}
45	Gating mechanisms on residual connections have been explored for adaptive depth \citep{elbayad2020depth}, mixture of experts \citep{shazeer2017moe}, and layer-selective computation. Our work applies per-layer scalar gating specifically to steer attention toward goal tokens, differing from prior work in objective (attention recovery rather than efficiency).
46	
47	\paragraph{Attention steering and intervention.}
48	Methods for steering attention include attention supervision \citep{liu2016attn}, auxiliary attention losses, and inference-time intervention \citep{li2023iti}. Our work connects to the exposure bias literature \citep{ranzato2016sequence, bengio2015scheduled}: teacher-forcing creates distributional mismatch between training and inference, which we show specifically disrupts auxiliary attention loss generalization.
